(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-blocks-ventricular-arrhythmias-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Arrhythmias II: Blocks and Ventriculars Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which causes should be checked first in a patient with frequent premature ventricular contractions?","choices":["Hypertension, diabetes","Dehydration, fever","Hypoxemia, electrolytes","Hyperlipidemia, obesity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Frequent premature ventricular contractions should prompt a search for their named causes: hypoxemia and electrolyte imbalance (hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, hypercalcemia). These beats occur with or without structural heart disease, and alcohol, caffeine and stimulants are other triggers to ask about. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Sudden cardiac death is defined as unexpected natural death from cardiac causes within what time of collapse?","choices":["Ten minutes","One hour","Six hours","Thirty days"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sudden cardiac death is generally defined as unexpected natural death from cardiac causes that occurs within 1 hour of the patient's collapse. The arrest comes from ventricular fibrillation, pulseless ventricular tachycardia, pulseless electrical activity or asystole, and coronary artery disease is the most common predisposing condition. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"After transcutaneous pacing stabilizes a patient in third-degree (complete) atrioventricular block, which is the definitive treatment?","choices":["Wearable defibrillator","Permanent pacemaker","Oral beta blocker","Catheter ablation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In complete block no P waves conduct, so this patient depends on a slow ventricular escape rhythm, often under 40. Transcutaneous pacing (with atropine or epinephrine, both controversial) is the emergent bridge, and a permanent pacemaker is the definitive treatment. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Once an advanced airway is in place during cardiac arrest, how are breaths given with continuous compressions?","choices":["One breath every 2 seconds","Compressions without breaths","One breath every 6 seconds","Two breaths per 30 compressions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Once an advanced airway such as an endotracheal tube is in place, cardiopulmonary resuscitation becomes continuous chest compressions with 1 breath every 6 seconds (10 breaths per minute). Compressions no longer pause for breaths, which keeps interruptions to a minimum. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which electrolyte is given as an intravenous push and then as an infusion to break torsades de pointes?","choices":["Magnesium","Potassium","Calcium","Phosphate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a conscious patient, intravenous magnesium is given to break torsades de pointes, followed by more magnesium as an infusion. Amiodarone, procainamide and beta blockers are avoided because they prolong the QT, and lidocaine is the only antiarrhythmic that can be used. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which degenerative, fibrotic disease of the conduction system is a named cause of left bundle branch block?","choices":["Lyme carditis","Chagas disease","Takotsubo syndrome","Lenègre-Lev disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Degenerative or fibrotic disease of the conduction system, called Lenègre-Lev disease, is a named cause of left bundle branch block. The other listed causes are chronic hypertension, chronic ischemic heart disease and severe coronary artery disease, dilated non-ischemic cardiomyopathy and valvular disease, especially aortic stenosis. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which finding defines hemodynamic instability in ventricular tachycardia?","choices":["Altered mental status","Heart rate of 130","Mild palpitations","Wide QRS complexes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In ventricular tachycardia, instability is defined by any one of chest pain, altered mental status, shortness of breath or hypotension. Confusion therefore makes the rhythm unstable, and an unstable monomorphic rhythm with a pulse gets synchronized cardioversion rather than antiarrhythmic drugs. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which treatment is used for symptomatic left bundle branch block with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction?","choices":["Intravenous magnesium","Catheter ablation","Biventricular pacemaker","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Left bundle branch block is usually left untreated; the exception is symptomatic block with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, which is treated with a biventricular pacer. Patients who become symptomatic usually have severe underlying heart disease and tolerate the block poorly. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic can be used in torsades de pointes?","choices":["Intravenous amiodarone","Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous metoprolol","Intravenous lidocaine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Intravenous lidocaine is the one antiarrhythmic that can be used in torsades de pointes, though the evidence for it is limited. Magnesium remains the main treatment, while amiodarone, procainamide and beta blockers are avoided because each prolongs the QT directly or indirectly. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which is the management of asymptomatic Mobitz type I block?","choices":["No treatment needed","Permanent pacemaker now","Transcutaneous pacing","Intravenous procainamide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type I (Wenckebach) block is usually asymptomatic and needs no treatment unless there are debilitating or heart failure symptoms. A permanent pacemaker is considered only for symptomatic patients. Common causes include vagal tone, beta blockers, nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers and digoxin. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which electrocardiogram finding defines first-degree atrioventricular block?","choices":["P waves unrelated to the QRS","Constant PR interval over 0.20 seconds","PR lengthening until a QRS drops","Constant PR with random dropped QRS"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. First-degree atrioventricular block is a prolonged PR interval, greater than 0.20 seconds and constant, with one P wave for every QRS and a narrow QRS under 0.12 seconds. The rhythm is regular, and the prolonged PR can occur along with an underlying rhythm. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How does Mobitz type I block usually present?","choices":["With palpitations in the neck","With heart failure symptoms","Without any symptoms","With fainting spells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type I (Wenckebach) block is usually asymptomatic. When symptoms appear, typically when exertion induces the block, they are dizziness, fatigue, exertional intolerance and syncope, and a symptomatic patient is considered for a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which medication is a named cause of first-degree atrioventricular block?","choices":["Digoxin","Furosemide","Amlodipine","Lisinopril"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin is a named medication cause of first-degree atrioventricular block, along with beta blockers and nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers. The block shows as a constant PR interval over 0.20 seconds, and most patients have no symptoms. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which mechanism makes atrioventricular block more common in elderly patients?","choices":["Shrinking atrial size","Degenerative conduction disease","Rising vagal tone with age","Age-related Lyme exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Age-related degenerative change of the conduction system is the most common cause of first-degree atrioventricular block, which is why block rises with age. The same degenerative disease, often with chronic hypertension and diabetes, underlies Mobitz type II and complete block. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What does the treatment of right bundle branch block depend on?","choices":["The rsR' amplitude","The underlying condition","The QRS width alone","The patient's heart rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Right bundle branch block is usually asymptomatic, and its treatment depends on the underlying condition, such as ischemic heart disease, pulmonary hypertension, a pulmonary embolism or cardiomyopathy. Prognosis likewise depends on the underlying heart disease, and new block can occur with no apparent heart disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"For a low-risk patient with symptomatic premature ventricular contractions, which treatment is first-line?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Implantable defibrillator","Beta blocker","Amiodarone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For a low-risk patient with symptoms from premature ventricular contractions, a beta blocker is first-line. Catheter ablation is held for symptoms refractory to it or a patient who cannot tolerate drug therapy, while low-risk patients with a low burden and no symptoms get reassurance and trigger removal. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"After defibrillation for polymorphic ventricular tachycardia, which procedure is urgent?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Pericardiocentesis","Exercise stress test","Coronary angiography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia is never truly stable, so it is defibrillated and followed by urgent coronary angiography with possible revascularization, because acute myocardial ischemia is its most common cause when the QT is normal. Intravenous metoprolol or amiodarone may be added. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Because Mobitz type II block usually originates at the bundle of His, what is true of its escape rhythm?","choices":["Sinus driven","Above 60 per minute","Weak and unreliable","Fast and narrow"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type II usually originates at the bundle of His, so the escape rhythm is weak and unreliable. That is why it carries a higher risk of progression to complete heart block and sudden cardiac death, is considered dangerous even without symptoms, and is treated definitively with a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which arrest rhythms carry the best prognosis?","choices":["Fibrillation, asystole","Tachycardia, pulseless electrical","Ventricular tachycardia, fibrillation","Asystole, pulseless electrical"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ventricular tachycardia and ventricular fibrillation are the arrest rhythms with the best prognosis. Asystole and pulseless electrical activity, by contrast, are unshockable, carry a poor prognosis and are usually end-of-life rhythms treated with immediate compressions and epinephrine. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which electrical treatment is delivered for polymorphic ventricular tachycardia?","choices":["Transcutaneous pacing","Drugs first, shock later","Unsynchronized defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia is never truly stable, even when the patient looks fine, because it is a short-lived transitional state. It is treated with defibrillation (unsynchronized cardioversion), followed by urgent coronary angiography with possible revascularization. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Into which two fascicles does the left bundle branch divide?","choices":["Anterior and posterior fascicles","Right and left fascicles","Inner and outer fascicles","Medial and apical fascicles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Below the bundle of His the conduction system splits into right and left bundle branches, and the left bundle branch divides into an anterior and a posterior fascicle. Interruption in either fascicle is a fascicular block, one form of intraventricular conduction delay. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which role does non-sustained ventricular tachycardia alone play in the decision to place an implantable defibrillator?","choices":["Reason enough for one","A contraindication to one","Needs a wearable vest first","Not an indication by itself"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Non-sustained ventricular tachycardia in and of itself is not an indication for an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD), though some patients qualify for primary prevention of sudden cardiac death related to sustained tachycardia. Symptomatic runs are treated with a beta blocker, then ablation or an antiarrhythmic. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which complication of bacterial endocarditis is a named cause of third-degree (complete) atrioventricular block?","choices":["Splinter hemorrhages","Septic lung emboli","Glomerulonephritis","An associated abscess"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bacterial endocarditis with an associated abscess is a named cause of third-degree (complete) block, alongside degenerative conduction disease, Lyme carditis, Chagas disease, myocarditis, amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, myocardial infarction and ablation. Complete block is treated definitively with a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"When is catheter ablation used for low-risk patients with symptomatic premature ventricular contractions?","choices":["When a beta blocker fails","As the very first step","When the burden is under 1%","Whenever bigeminy appears"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In low-risk patients with symptomatic premature ventricular contractions, a beta blocker is first-line, and catheter ablation is used when symptoms are refractory to it or the patient cannot tolerate drug therapy. Ablation comes first only in a high-risk patient with isolated premature-beat cardiomyopathy and heart failure signs. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"When left bundle branch block does cause symptoms, what do they resemble?","choices":["Asthma attack","Panic attack","Hypoglycemia","Heart failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Most patients with left bundle branch block are asymptomatic. Those who develop symptoms usually have severe underlying heart disease and tolerate the block poorly, so the picture resembles a congestive heart failure exacerbation: dizziness, shortness of breath and dyspnea on exertion. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"For premature ventricular contractions under 1% of beats with no structural heart disease and no symptoms, which is the plan?","choices":["Lifelong beta blocker","Catheter ablation","Implantable defibrillator","Reassure and remove triggers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A low burden (under 1% of beats or 1,000 a day), no structural heart disease and no symptoms place this patient in the lowest-risk group, so the plan is reassurance and eliminating triggers such as alcohol, caffeine and stimulants. Drugs or ablation are kept for symptoms or high-risk features. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"For hemodynamically stable, sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia, which approach is used?","choices":["Intravenous adenosine","Intravenous antiarrhythmic","Immediate defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This patient is hemodynamically stable with a monomorphic sustained rhythm, so after a monitor, intravenous access, a 12-lead electrocardiogram, oxygen and labs, the rhythm is pharmacologically cardioverted with intravenous lidocaine, amiodarone or procainamide. Procainamide is avoided with a prolonged QT or heart failure. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which infiltrative diseases are named causes of third-degree (complete) atrioventricular block?","choices":["Asthma and emphysema","Amyloidosis and sarcoidosis","Cirrhosis and pancreatitis","Osteoporosis and gout"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Inflammatory and infiltrative disease (myocarditis, amyloidosis and sarcoidosis) is a named cause of complete heart block, alongside degenerative conduction disease, Lyme carditis, Chagas disease, endocarditis with an abscess, myocardial infarction and ablation. Treatment is pacing, then a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which finding in lead V1 is expected in left bundle branch block?","choices":["Dominant S wave","rsR' pattern","Delta wave","Tall, narrow R"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In left bundle branch block the left ventricle is activated by current crossing the septum from the right side, so V1 shows a dominant S wave (an rS pattern). The other criteria are a QRS over 0.12 seconds, an R-wave peak over 0.06 seconds, a monophasic R in the lateral leads and no Q waves in I, V5 and V6. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"In a high-risk patient with premature ventricular contractions but no isolated beat-induced cardiomyopathy, what is the first step?","choices":["Beta blocker, optimize disease","Reassurance and follow-up","Immediate defibrillation","Intravenous magnesium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. High-risk features include a burden over 15%, premature-beat cardiomyopathy, structural or conduction disease and prior syncope. Ablation comes first only for isolated premature-beat cardiomyopathy with heart failure signs; otherwise, the first step is a beta blocker plus optimized treatment of the underlying cardiac disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"}]};
